<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Osnabrück</h1><p class="st-meta">1225 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Osnabrück</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Westphalian church state where the Peace of Westphalia was negotiated and where, uniquely, Catholic and Lutheran bishops alternated after 1648.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> founded the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> around 780 after his conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> acquired estates and comital rights between the Ems and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, and in the 13th century, around 1225, they emerged as imperial princes ruling a consolidated territory free of the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tecklenburg">Tecklenburg</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The cathedral chapter and estates held strong rights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> and Münster hosted the negotiations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>. Under its terms the see alternated between a Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and a Lutheran prince of the house of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-elector-of-hanover">Ernest Augustus</a>, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-great-britain">George I of Great Britain</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-frederick-duke-of-york-and-albany">Frederick, Duke of York</a>, held it. The territory was known for linen and for the writer and statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justus-moser">Justus Möser</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the see was secularised and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. After the Napoleonic interlude it remained Hanoverian until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a> in 1866. The Catholic diocese continues.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-osnabruck.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Osnabrück" width="320" height="412"><figcaption>Image: BmMuenster_1786.jpg: *Central_europe_1786.jpg: en:William R. Shepherd derivative work: Beckstet (talk) derivative wor... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hochstift_Osnabr%C3%BCck_1786.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1225 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Osnabrück</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Franz Wilhelm von Wartenberg, Ernest Augustus, Frederick, Duke of York</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Westphalian Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Osnabrück</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1514&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-osnabruck" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Osnabrück on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4863 373 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Osnabrück lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5183 629L5229 694M5183 629L5219 626"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5219 626h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5183 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5183 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1514&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-osnabruck">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
